<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Warsaw</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Warsaw is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> River. It became the royal residence and effective capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-iii-vasa">Sigismund III Vasa</a> moved the court there from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krakow">Kraków</a> around 1596, and from 1569 it was the regular seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sejm">Sejm</a> and of royal elections. Accounts of the Commonwealth discuss it in the context of the state's foundation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/warsaw.jpg" alt="Warsaw" width="320" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Emptywords · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aleja_Niepdleglosci_Warsaw_2022_aerial_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…a Polish mission led by Kazimierz Michałowski before the area was flooded by Lake Nasser in the 1960s, are now in the National Museum in <b>Warsaw</b> and the Sudan National Museum in Khartoum and show bishops, kings and saints in a style that blends Byzantine and local traditions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/makuria">Makuria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…which the king was chosen by the entire szlachta nobility in mass elections, usually held at Wola outside <b>Warsaw</b>; a bicameral Sejm representing noble interests across both constituent states; and a guarantee of religious peace, the Warsaw Confederation of 1573, which made the Commonwealth a refuge for religious…</blockquote><a href="/empire/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In Poland, when the Jagiellonian line ended with the death of Sigismund II Augustus in 1572, the nobles decided that every nobleman who came to the election field near <b>Warsaw</b> should vote.</blockquote><a href="/government/elective-monarchy">Elective monarchy · Through History</a></li><li><blockquote>Since May 2024 the third-oldest manuscript version, from 1456, has been on permanent display in the Palace of the Commonwealth in <b>Warsaw</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/bogurodzica">Bogurodzica (Mother of God)</a></li><li><blockquote>When Dąbrowski and Wybicki arrived in Poznań on 6 November 1806, crowds greeted them with the song, and it became one of the most popular patriotic songs of the Duchy of <b>Warsaw</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/poland-is-not-yet-lost">Poland Is Not Yet Lost</a></li><li><blockquote>During the German occupation in the Second World War, on the eve of Independence Day, 11 November 1939, scouts in Zielonka near <b>Warsaw</b> put up posters bearing the poem; in reprisal the occupiers executed nine scouts and other inhabitants of the town.</blockquote><a href="/songs/rota">Rota</a></li></ul></section>
